A||||4||Steven||It's rebuild time!
Z||000000||Steven||09-02-99||09:15 PM||||Now I have seen a lot of info on stroking an engine to 3.1L. The stoke engine is still running... not as strong, but still running. I went out and bought a 2.8L Fiero engine that needs some love'n. Looking to spend about $1100 add $600 more for headers. Can anyone tell me how should I spend the first $1100, how do I call for parts, and what are the part numbers?
Z||000001||batboy||09-03-99||05:18 AM||||This subject was been discussed in great detail a number of times. Maybe look through some of the past posts for the general info and ask about specific things you don't know from there. A lot of the initial time and money will be spent taking the block and heads to a good auto machine shop. They need to cleaned and checked for cracks, install new cam bearings, bore and deck the block if necessary, the heads need to be checked for straightness and valve guides replaced if needed, and of course the valves and seats need ground. All this prep work can get expensive, but it needs to be done.
Z||000002||Steven||09-03-99||10:26 PM||||Well to tell the truth then, I am a little concern as to brand and model of cam to start with. Two of the bigger companies have cams that they say will work. Comp Cams said to use their 16-232-4 when upgrading the ECM, and the converter removed. Crane has a cam (can't find number at this time) which the Fiero Store seems to be pushing. Now I feel that I have read just about most the information out on cams, and the one thing that every article stated was the cam was hard thing to master and not just any old bump stick will do. What I was hoping for was some feed back from anyone how might have used either of these, and how they liked the proformance.
Z||000003||batboy||09-04-99||12:08 AM||||Shaun, Dan-o, and several others I can't think of off hand are using higher lift cams in their V6's. Hopefully they will tell you how they like them. The GM Hi-po cam and the very similar Crane CompuCam are two I can think of that will give you a nice power gain, but will still work with the ECM. I'm not sure about the Comp Cam, but I have used their cams in several of my V8, they're a good company.<p>I am puzzled about your comment referring to removing the converter. If it's the torque converter you mean, you can't just remove it. What the cam people probably meant was that you have to replace it with a higher stall speed converter, which is expensive even if you can find one for the Fiero tranny. 
Z||000004||Steven||09-04-99||09:35 PM||||I am sorry. Should have said Catalytic convertor. 
